# CSS Container Queries — Responsive Design Based on Parent Elements

## 1. What Are Container Queries?

Traditionally, developers use CSS Media Queries to change layouts depending on the screen or viewport size.

But what if a component needs to change its layout based on the size of its parent container rather than the entire screen?

That's where **CSS Container Queries** come in.

Container Queries allow an element to apply different CSS styles depending on the size of its containing element.

In simple words:

- Media Queries ask: How wide is the screen?
- Container Queries ask: How much space does this component have?

## 2. Why Are Container Queries Useful?

Imagine a product card displayed in two places:

- A large section on a desktop page.
- A narrow sidebar on the same desktop page.

The screen size hasn't changed, but the available space for the card has.

Media Queries alone cannot easily handle this component-level requirement.

Container Queries allow the card to adapt independently to its available space.

Common use cases:
- Reusable UI components.
- Product cards.
- Dashboard widgets.
- Sidebar layouts.
- Responsive navigation components.
- Component-based design systems.

## 3. Understanding the Basic Syntax

Container Queries require two main steps.

**Step 1: Define a container**

```css
.card-container {
    container-type: inline-size;
}
```

This tells the browser that the element can act as a query container for its inline size.

**Step 2: Write a container query**

```css
@container (min-width: 500px) {
    .card {
        display: flex;
    }
}
```

The styles inside the query apply when the nearest eligible query container is at least 500px wide.

## 4. A Complete Example

Let's create a responsive profile card.

```html
<div class="profile-container">
    <div class="profile-card">
        <img src="profile.jpg" alt="Profile photo">

        <div class="profile-info">
            <h2>Nikitha</h2>
            <p>Computer Science Student</p>
            <button>View Profile</button>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
```

### CSS

```css
.profile-container {
    container-type: inline-size;
}

.profile-card {
    display: block;
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    border-radius: 12px;
}

.profile-card img {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 150px;
    border-radius: 50%;
}

@container (min-width: 450px) {
    .profile-card {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 20px;
    }
}
```

### What happens?

- When the container is narrower than 450px, the card uses a vertical layout.
- When the container reaches at least 450px, the card changes to a horizontal layout.

The component adapts to its own container instead of depending on the browser window.

## 5. Understanding Different Container Types

CSS provides different container types.

| Container type | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `normal` | Default behavior; does not establish a size query container |
| `inline-size` | Allows queries based on inline size |
| `size` | Allows queries based on both width and height |

Example:

```css
.container {
    container-type: size;
}
```

**Important:** Size containment affects how the container's dimensions are calculated. Use it carefully because the container may need an explicitly established size.

## 6. Named Container Queries

What if a page has multiple containers?

You can give containers names to target a particular one.

```css
.sidebar {
    container-type: inline-size;
    container-name: sidebar;
}

@container sidebar (min-width: 300px) {
    .widget {
        display: flex;
    }
}
```

Here, the query specifically targets the nearest eligible container named `sidebar`.

You can also combine the declarations:

```css
.sidebar {
    container: sidebar / inline-size;
}
```

## 7. Container Queries vs Media Queries

| Feature | Media Queries | Container Queries |
|---|---|---|
| Based on | Viewport or device conditions | Container dimensions |
| Main purpose | Page-level responsiveness | Component-level responsiveness |
| Depends on screen size? | Commonly, yes | Not necessarily |
| Best use | Overall page layout | Reusable component layouts |
| Syntax | `@media` | `@container` |

Both are useful and can work together in the same project.

## 8. Understanding Container Query Units

CSS also provides special units that are relative to a query container.

| Unit | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `cqw` | 1% of the query container's width |
| `cqh` | 1% of the query container's height |
| `cqi` | 1% of the query container's inline size |
| `cqb` | 1% of the query container's block size |
| `cqmin` | Smaller of `cqi` and `cqb` |
| `cqmax` | Larger of `cqi` and `cqb` |

Example:

```css
.card-title {
    font-size: 5cqi;
}
```

The font size is based on 5% of the query container's inline size.

## 9. Common Mistakes

- Forgetting to define `container-type` before using a size query.
- Assuming Container Queries automatically respond to viewport changes.
- Using `@container` without understanding which ancestor is the query container.
- Confusing `inline-size` with `size`.
- Using container query units without an eligible query container.
- Assuming Container Queries completely replace Media Queries.

## 10. Key Takeaways

- Container Queries make components responsive to their parent container's size.
- `container-type: inline-size` enables inline-size queries.
- `@container` defines the conditions for applying styles.
- Named containers help target specific containers.
- Container query units allow sizing based on container dimensions.
- Container Queries and Media Queries solve different responsive design problems.

## One-Line Summary

**CSS Container Queries allow reusable components to adapt to the space available in their parent container rather than relying only on the browser viewport.**
